Date: Fri, 17 Sep 93 03:57:53 BST
From: Mark Grant <Mark.Grant@isltd.insignia.com>
Subject: GUNZ: "Pro-gun" is not equal to "pro-violence" (long)

Andy Lowton said the following, and this reply is primarily directed to him, 
one 'Brit' to another (thought some of you might be interested too, though) :

> As a Brit, i am coming at this from a different perspective to the 
> vast majority of you.  As you are all probably aware, the ownership
> of guns is much more restricted over here.  Previously I have always
> been of the opinion that 'more guns means more people get shot'.

And, in fact the British experience bears this out. With about 5% of the
per-capita firearms ownership of the US (legal and illegal), there are very 
few people shot to death over here. Instead, they are typically stabbed to 
death, or have their brains smashed out of their skull with a blunt object. In 
a recent survey, 99% of all murdered people stated that they really didn't see 
any difference, though if they had to die they'd prefer to have gone quickly 
with a bullet (Well, actually, I made that survey up, but hopefully you see the 
point).

The murder rate in Britain is about 1/4 of the rate in the US, on the other
hand, the suicide rate is rather higher. The most likely cause for the lower
murder rate is the difference in drug laws - in Britain being caught with
small amounts of drugs will probably result in a caution rather than a
prison sentence with no parole and the loss of your house and posessions,
also as we don't have the no-parole laws, we don't release violent criminals
to make space for drug users. Of course, in the regions with large amounts 
of high-profile drug 'crime' the murder rate is also high (e.g. Manchester).

I don't know about the rest of the country, but ignoring the terrorist 
incidents, the violent crime rate in London is about the same as the US 
average, the robbery rate over the country as a whole is of a similar 
order to the figures someone quoted for highly controlled areas of the US, 
and although the fraction of that attributable to armed robbery with guns is 
rather lower than the US, that number of such robberies each year doubled 
between 1988 (when the 'ban-those-scary-assault-rifles' law was passed) and 
1991, and has been claimed to have doubled again since (I haven't seen any 
official figures, but there have been such claims in newspapers attributed to 
the police). In London, the annual number of armed robberies with guns has
increased by about a factor of 50 since 1920 when the first gun control law 
was passed.

I should also add that the original reason claimed for passing the 1920
law was crime control. Only when the cabinet papers were declassified did
it turn out that the government were in fact more worried about the 
possibility of a communist revolution. The first official review of the gun 
laws by a group of MPs a decade or so later actually recommended removing 
controls from a variety of guns, but these recommendations were overturned
by the cabinet. The only real result of the laws was that in 1940 when there
was a real prospect of a German invasion, there were nowhere near enough guns 
to go round (as, indeed Winston Churchill mentioned).

I've noticed in the last few days a lot of publicity over here about the
British tourist who was shot in Florida. Well, there were a couple of German
tourists shot dead in Scotland a few months back, and I didn't notice
a flurry of news stories about that, or a huge number of German tourists
refusing to come to Britain, or claiming that Britain should do something
about its violent crime and take all possible measures to protect tourists 
(which, of course we ought to be doing anyway).

It also completely ignores the fact that Florida's murder rate dropped by
20% from 1986-1991 after they introduced their law allowing all law-abiding
citizens to carry concealed weapons (compared to a 14% increase over the
US as a whole in that time). The reason tourists get shot is because they
are the only easily identifiable group in the state who don't (usually)
have guns. The best way to reduce the chance of them being murdered would
be to give concealed-carry permits for the duration of their stay to all 
those who could demonstrate safe gun handling (e.g. anyone who already had 
a UK firearms licence or shotgun certificate).

> However,
> reading this thread has persuaded me of the argument that rapists etc
> would think twice if they suspected that the potential victim was
> carrying a gun.

Someone posted a list of the per-capita incidence of rape in various
countries from the mid-eighties on Usenet recently, which showed the rate 
in Britain was then about twice that in the US. Not too suprising, really. 
Top of the list was Australia at almost four times the US rate (and with 
tough anti-handgun laws).

Time Out had an article on self-defence for women a week or two ago, which
basically said that if someone attacked you you should either scratch
them with a key (since carrying anything specifically for self-defence
and no other purpose is against the law in this country), or give in and
let them rob you, or rape you or kill you or whatever. Dunno about you, but
personally, given the choice between a key and a magnum, I'd take the magnum 
any day.

Or as Handgunner magazine used to say "When Ladies carried guns, Gentlemen
were Gentlemen", referring to the days when a ladies hand-warmer or a
gentleman's suit would almost always come with a pocket specifically intended
for a pistol (back before inside-belt and shoulder holsters).

> One thing still bothers me though.  If you regularly go to pubs (sorry 
> bars), there are very often fights between people who have had 'one over
> the eight'.  I suppose you can still use the argument that people would
> not use a gun in this situation, but when your drunk, you're not
> rational.  We also have a problem over here called football hooliganism,
> I dread to think what would happen if two sets of armed supporters met
> in a crowded city centre.

Ever been to Manchester ? They like shooting members of opposing gangs up
there.

Actually, I doubt that the average football hooligan is likely to be armed
with a gun in the first place. Even in states like Florida, only a tiny
fraction of the people eligible for concealed-carry permits actually have
them, but that's still plenty enough to significantly reduce the crime
rates. Also, I think they have laws against carrying guns in bars, which
makes a fair amount of sense. As long as the barman's got his trusty shotgun
(or Uzi, or whatever) under the counter, you might just as well hand yours
in at the door.

You could equally suggest that today's hooligans ought to be armed with
'Rambo knives' rather than Stanley knives. They're not, because a Stanley
knife will scar someone for life, but is unlikely to kill them, and they
prefer their fun to be safe. They don't want to end up in a coffin or
in the dock on a murder charge. Having said that, there were those cases
in the eighties when large numbers of people were crushed to death during
fighting on the terraces, but I think that's a different matter.

> Am I right in thinking that the general attitude of the people on this list
> would be that once a few of these idiots were killed (and maybe a couple
> of innocent bystanders as well) then the majority of the rest of them
> would think it was not worth it? Or is it that they will all kill each 
> other in the end?  Keep in mind that these people are complete morons
> and not very rational.

Politically incorrect as the opinion may be, I really don't care about
a few morons shooting each other. I'd happily trade a few hundred dead
football hooligans each year for a halving of the rape and robbery rates. I
don't see any way of reducing them other than by giving people back the
means of self-defence and the legal ability to defend themselves.

> Like I said earlier, this is not an anti-gun post, just the nagging doubts
> of a Brit who is not used to these issues.

And this is from another Brit who is, both as an ex-shooter over here (gave
up due to the 1988 laws) and a regular visitor to Florida (where, fortunately, 
I have several friends who DO carry guns). Gun control creates a climate where
strong criminals are free to do what they want to the law-abiding amongst us,
who can do nothing in return. 

Did you see the newspaper article recently which claimed that burglars in 
Britain have started breaking into houses when they were occupied because 
they prefer to tackle the unarmed occupants than deal with a burglar alarm ?
That's what gun control gets you, not a safe, well-behaved, polite society. In
my experience, America is a much more polite society than Britain, and if
you avoid the inner cities and drug dealers (and cops), it's a safer society 
as well.

				Mark

