Date: Thu, 16 Sep 93 22:10:31 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: "Hey, ::exclude You, Too!"

I am wondering about the wisdom, and even the flamish aspects, of the
various "I've ::excluded you, so I won't have to read your drivel"
posts I'm seeing since my return to the List. Sometimes this "I don't
see foo's posts because I have ::excluded him" message gets repeated
several times, thus becoming a _kind_ of insult. 

Truly, the ::exclude feature is useful, just as "kill" files are on
Usenet.

But when people publically announce their ::exclude intentions, and go
on to explain why, doesn't that subvert the purpose, which is to
ruduce flaming by removing sources of irritation?

Me, I prefer that the targets of my ::exclude--if there are any, and
I'm not saying one way or another--have no idea I have excluded them!
Perhaps this shows I am in some sense more malicious than these
friends and colleagues of mine who announce their ::excludes (or at
least some of them).

It's not a big deal, this announcing of ::excludes, but it has the
potential of becoming a technique for insulting rather than for
reducing acrimony.

And there's no denying the verb is entering our Extropian language. Can it
be long before I'm at a party chattering away and I hear someone say
"Colon colon exclude Tim"?

And wouldn't it be a delicious irony--some of you will know what I'm
talking about--if this made it into some future edition of "The
Hackers Dictionary"?


