Date: Thu, 16 Sep 93 23:46:51 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Proposal for Paying for Advanced List Features

Extropiates (devotees of narcocapitalism, natch!),

I initially intended to send this letter to Ray Cromwell, but have
decided to copy the whole List on it. While I have not yet felt the
need to use the new commands in the List software, I recognize the
importance of software like this as we move into and colonize
cyberspace. Eventually we'll have digital money, more powerful agents,
and "solid" walls in cyberspace--I like to point out to people that
crypto is the only "mortar" we'll have to make the walls and doors and
whatnot in cyberspace have solidity, security, permanence--but for now
we have to start with software like we have here.

I think a real subscription service is needed. Let anyone onto the main,
feature-deprived list, but only provide advanced services to those
committed enough to the cause to at least join ExI, and perhaps to
separately contribute money to the List software itself.

Here's what I wrote:


Ray, 

Excellent work you're doing. You could further develop it and sell it
(or "shareware" it, or GNU license it, whatever), as a kind of
"instant list software" package. ("majordomo" I hear a lot about, but
I think it is mainly for subscription requests and the like.)

Meanwhile, send me your mailing address and I will send you a check
for $30 (in Fraudulent Reserve Notes, alas) to help things further.

I believe, in fact, that one option to consider is only allowing folks
who have paid (to you, to the List, to ExI, some combination to be
determined) to have access to the *full features* of the list. 

- let anyone get on the list "the old-fashioned way," the simple echo
of all messages (this mode is needed as a "loss leader" to recruit new
or casual users). No charge for this.

- for more advanced features to be turned on, subscribers must
literally "subscribe." This would include digests, the "::" commands,
etc. (The pricing depends on a lot of things, and what people are
willing to pay, etc. For starters, $30 a year sounds about right.)

- eventually, services could be on an "a la carte" basis, with various
fees for various agents and commands. Initially, this would be too
much of a bookkeeping headache, so the simple "Member" vs.
"Non-Member" classification would be best.

- the "Member" status could be part of the user database (it has to
be, to enable the advanced features). This could also allow folks to
do things like "::exclude nonmembers" . (The use of this might be to
::exclude posts from people who have not joined the ExI, who don't
read "Extropy," and hence who are very likely to "not know the
basics." I'm not saying this is a good thing to do, just an example.)

If 100 subscribers paid even as little as $30 a year (in PBS begathon
jargon: "That's only 8 cents a day, folks!"), this would generate
$3000 a year. Not a fortune, but enough to pay for more list software
development, time on "panix," etc. I figure this would buy a few
hundred hours of your time, Ray.

And unlike the PBS begathons, which are beaming electromagnetic
signals out there for anyone to pick up for free (though they use the same
"implied contract" language our friend Perry recently used in his
repudiation of the post office scam), the List software can actually
be said to be part of a *real* contract, not an implied one. You pays
your money, you get more features.

Subscribing with real money (sorry, FRS notes) to the List is a lot
more realistic, and tangible, than the nice ideas--which are only
ideas--about paying for specific posts, or paying based on reputation,
etc. Those may come later, but for now a simple "Member/Nonmember"
dichotomy is very easy to manage.

It also incentivizes the other "agent writers" out there to make their
software availability contingent on subscriptions. Thus,
Brian/Rowan/Holt Hawthorne (sorry if I didn't get his name right) is
motivated to make "HeX" meet some standards, because people will have
to decide if they really want to pay $5 a year, or whatever, to
"enable" certain commands at the List headquarters site so as to use
HeX. (For all I know of HeX, he processes everything through his own
site and doesn't use the ExI site...this was just an example of
someone adding custom agents into the suite of existing ExI agents and
commands.)

There's the issue of Internet commercialization, of asking
subscribers to pay. I don't know if any mailing lists are on such a
paid basis. Could be an interesting case.

Brad Templeton's "ClariNet" is a fee-based system, so my guess is that
it's legal.

And this scheme might also fly by dint of the entire list being
available to anyone, with only "specialized services" being paid for.

Let me know what you think.


-Tim


